1. Introduction to Project Sprint
A Project Sprint is a short, structured development period in which a team or individual works on building a functional project within a specific time.

Project Sprint I – Build focuses on converting an idea into a working Python application using programming concepts learned earlier.

The main objective is to develop a basic but functional project.

2. Objectives of Project Sprint I
Understand real-world problems.

Develop logical thinking and problem-solving skills.

Apply Python programming concepts practically.

Learn to write and execute Python programs.

Develop a working project.

Identify and correct programming errors.

3. Steps Involved in Project Development
Problem Identification
Identify a real-world problem that can be solved using Python.

Planning
Decide the project's objectives, features and requirements.

Designing
Prepare the algorithm, flowchart and program structure.

Coding
Write the Python program using appropriate functions, variables, loops and conditions.

Testing
Execute the program and check whether it produces the expected output.

Debugging
Identify and correct errors in the program.

Final Implementation
Run the completed project and verify its functionality.

4. Important Python Concepts Used in Project Building
4.1 Variables
Variables are used to store data values in a program.

project_name = "Student Management"total_students = 50print(project_name)print(total_students)

4.2 Conditional Statements
Conditional statements execute different blocks of code depending on a condition.

marks = 75if marks >= 40:    print("Pass")else:    print("Fail")

4.3 Loops
Loops are used to execute a block of code repeatedly.

Example:

for i in range(1, 6):    print(i)

4.4 Functions
Functions are reusable blocks of code designed to perform specific tasks.

def add(a, b):    return a + bprint(add(10, 20))

4.5 Lists
Lists store multiple values in a single variable.

students = ["Amit", "Rahul", "Rohit"]for name in students:    print(name)

4.6 File Handling
File handling allows a Python program to create, read and write files.

file = open("data.txt", "w")file.write("Welcome to Python Project")file.close()

5. Example of a Mini Project
Student Marks Management System
Objective: To create a simple Python application that stores student names and calculates their total and average marks.

Program:

name = input("Enter student name: ")m1 = int(input("Enter marks in Subject 1: "))m2 = int(input("Enter marks in Subject 2: "))m3 = int(input("Enter marks in Subject 3: "))total = m1 + m2 + m3average = total / 3print("\n--- Student Report ---")print("Name:", name)print("Total Marks:", total)print("Average:", average)if average >= 40:    print("Result: Pass")else:    print("Result: Fail")

